.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
See the module.
.PP
\fBTree\fR: hierarchical data as indented rows with an arrow to open each branch, after Mantine's Tree, adapted to GNOME. The behavior is the \fBtree\fR primitive; this draws it and speaks \fBone\fR message, \fBEvent\fR, which the app applies with \fBTreeState::apply\fR:
.PP
.nf
.RS
// view
Tree::new(&self.data, &self.tree)
    .lines(true)
    .checkboxes(true)
    .highlight(&self.search)
    .on_event(Msg::Tree)

// update
Msg::Tree(event) => if let Some(Effect::Load(v)) = self.tree.apply(&mut self.data, event) { /* fetch v */ }
.RE
.fi
.PP
What Mantine's page shows is here: connecting \fBlines\fR, a \fBlevel_offset\fR, \fBcheckboxes\fR (with the indeterminate state of a half\-checked branch), \fBicons\fR and a \fBlabel\fR renderer (\fBrenderNode\fR), \fBhighlight\fRed matches, lazily loaded branches (a spinner while loading, the error and Retry when it failed), drag and drop (\fBdraggable\fR: by the row or by a grip, with \fBallow_drop\fR for the nodes that must not take a drop), and \fBwindow\fR for very large trees. Search and filtering stay outside, as in Mantine: filter the data with \fBtree::filter\fR and open the result with \fBTreeState::expand_all\fR.
.PP
\fBAdapted to GNOME.\fR GNOME has no tree\-view pattern in the HIG; the model is GTK's \fBGtkTreeExpander\fR: rows indent by depth, a leaf keeps the width of the arrow so its label lines up with its siblings' (\fBindent\-for\-icon\fR), the arrow points right when closed and down when open, and the keys are \fB+\fR \fB*\fR / \fB\-\fR \fB/\fR / Ctrl+Space and the arrows (the primitive's \fBnavigate\fR; not wired here, as iced has no focus model for it). A selected row is the neutral flat\-button highlight, as in libadwaita's sidebars; a drop shows as the accent line and tint of the reorderable list. For small static hierarchies the HIG's own answer is an \fBExpanderRow\fR in a boxed list.
